{
  "dom_id": "step-34865",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Modeling with Inverse Square Laws",
  "problem": "The irradiance $I,$ in $\\mathrm{W}/m^2,$ of a lamp placed at the center of a spherical chamber varies inversely with the **square** of the radius $r$ of the chamber, measured in meters. It is known that $I = 0.75\\,\\mathrm{W}/m^2$ when $r = 1.2\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What equation gives the relationship between $I$ and $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the irradiance $I$ is inversely proportional to the square of the radius $r$ of the chamber, we must have\n\n$$\nI= \\dfrac {k}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $I=0.75\\,\\mathrm{W}/m^2$ when $r=1.2\\,\\textrm{m}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75 & =\\frac{k}{{1.2}^{2}} \\\\ 0.75 & =\\frac{k}{1.44} \\\\ 0.75\\cdot 1.44 & =k \\\\ 1.08 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\nI= \\dfrac{1.08}{r^2}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
